<div class="case-content"><section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">THE IDEA</p><h2>Make everyday money decisions easier.</h2><p>Sika is for people who want a clearer view of their finances without the complexity of a banking app. It connects transaction tracking, account balances, savings goals, recurring payments, receipt scanning, spending insights, and monthly recaps.</p><p>Its “Should I Buy?” assistant brings those pieces together to explain how a potential purchase fits the user’s financial context.</p></section>
<section class="case-section" id="agent-workflow"><p class="eyebrow">HOW I BUILT IT</p><h2>Agents in the workflow.<br />Ownership in the decisions.</h2><p>I initially built Sika with Claude, then paused development. In September 2026, I returned to the project using ChatGPT to revisit its architecture, introduce new features, and rework existing experiences.</p><p>The app was built entirely through AI-assisted development, with me directing the product, architecture, feature breakdown, and implementation. The web application uses Next.js, the native iOS app uses SwiftUI, and both share a Supabase backend.</p><p>That approach made iteration fast. It also made reviewing the results essential: a feature can work on screen while still behaving incorrectly within the product’s financial model.</p></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">A DIFFICULT FEATURE</p><h2>Reworking “Should I Buy?”</h2><p>A discontinued API subscription interrupted the existing AI feature. I used the migration from Anthropic to OpenAI to revisit both the integration and the advice it produced.</p><p>I directed the iOS app to a server-side decision endpoint, keeping provider credentials out of the client. The backend assembles context about the purchase, spending, savings goals, and recurring commitments before requesting a structured response.</p><ul><li><strong>Clearer advice:</strong> plain-language explanations of purchase impact, with less slang and fewer distracting calculations.</li><li><strong>Practical alternatives:</strong> a specific savings plan when waiting makes more sense.</li><li><strong>Better presentation:</strong> currency-aware formatting and improved error states.</li><li><strong>Context-aware responses:</strong> distinguishing early guidance from advice informed by more spending history.</li></ul></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">THE BROADER REVAMP</p><h2>Questioning the underlying behaviour.</h2><p>The review went beyond the AI integration. I revisited how Sika represents financial activity and how people move through the app.</p><ul><li><strong>Recurring payments:</strong> pending and skip states, so scheduled expenses are not automatically treated as completed transactions.</li><li><strong>Savings goals:</strong> same-account contributions treated as earmarked funds rather than artificial transfers.</li><li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Face ID reserved for genuine authentication events, rather than ordinary saves and refreshes.</li><li><strong>Receipt scanning:</strong> native iOS text recognition with a review step before a transaction is created.</li><li><strong>Everyday clarity:</strong> a balance privacy toggle, simpler language, and a Home screen that puts actionable items first.</li></ul></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT I TOOK AWAY</p><h2>Working code is the beginning.</h2><div class="case-callout"><p>The difficult work is validating whether a feature fits the product’s data model, privacy requirements, failure modes, and real user behaviour.</p></div><p>Sika is the clearest example of how I work with agents: define the intent, inspect what gets built, and keep refining the product decisions behind the implementation.</p><p>The web app is live on Vercel. The native iOS app is still in development.</p><div class="case-actions"><a class="text-link" href="https://sika-dlrl.vercel.app/">Visit the live web app <Icon name="external" /></a></div></section>
